WEBVTT

00:00.341 --> 00:01.281
[SPEAKER_02]: Hello, everyone.

00:01.581 --> 00:09.243
[SPEAKER_02]: Welcome back to English Podcast 1314, the best place to learn English and grow every day.

00:09.263 --> 00:12.824
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm Mia, and here is my great friend, Leo.

00:13.244 --> 00:13.704
[SPEAKER_02]: Hi, Leo.

00:13.924 --> 00:14.625
[SPEAKER_02]: How are you today?

00:14.645 --> 00:15.725
[SPEAKER_00]: Hey, Mia.

00:15.825 --> 00:16.565
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm doing great.

00:17.045 --> 00:19.286
[SPEAKER_00]: I have a lot of energy for today's topic.

00:19.586 --> 00:20.026
[SPEAKER_00]: How about you?

00:20.726 --> 00:21.486
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm doing well.

00:21.586 --> 00:21.946
[SPEAKER_00]: Thanks.

00:22.647 --> 00:24.187
[SPEAKER_00]: So what are we talking about today?

00:24.787 --> 00:28.488
[SPEAKER_02]: Today, we're talking about something simple but powerful.

00:29.045 --> 00:30.586
[SPEAKER_02]: Your daily routine.

00:31.346 --> 00:31.706
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

00:32.187 --> 00:36.208
[SPEAKER_00]: The small things we do every day can really influence how we feel.

00:36.909 --> 00:37.469
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

00:38.009 --> 00:41.191
[SPEAKER_02]: Your routine can make you feel calm or stressed.

00:41.691 --> 00:44.913
[SPEAKER_02]: It can also help you learn English step by step.

00:45.613 --> 00:49.895
[SPEAKER_00]: And the best part is you don't need a perfect life to have a good routine.

00:50.335 --> 00:51.916
[SPEAKER_00]: You just need small habits.

00:52.637 --> 00:53.157
[SPEAKER_02]: That's right.

00:53.537 --> 00:56.038
[SPEAKER_02]: In this episode, we'll share our routines.

00:56.418 --> 00:59.259
[SPEAKER_02]: Morning, afternoon, evening, and night.

00:59.860 --> 01:04.721
[SPEAKER_00]: We'll also give you easy English sentences you can copy and use in real life.

01:05.482 --> 01:07.543
[SPEAKER_02]: And we have something special today.

01:07.983 --> 01:11.084
[SPEAKER_02]: We made a free PDF to go with this episode.

01:11.744 --> 01:17.166
[SPEAKER_00]: It has the key ideas, simple questions, and a few grammar and speaking exercises.

01:17.926 --> 01:22.188
[SPEAKER_02]: So if you want extra practice, check the PDF after the episode.

01:22.826 --> 01:23.126
[SPEAKER_00]: Great.

01:23.666 --> 01:30.508
[SPEAKER_00]: Also, we'll use some useful words again and again, like focus, prioritize, and recharge.

01:31.328 --> 01:36.849
[SPEAKER_02]: And at the end, we'll do a vocabulary review with about 10 words from today's talk.

01:37.449 --> 01:37.849
[SPEAKER_00]: Perfect.

01:38.269 --> 01:39.610
[SPEAKER_00]: Ready to start with the morning?

01:40.450 --> 01:40.770
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes.

01:41.210 --> 01:46.971
[SPEAKER_02]: Let's begin with the morning routine, because mornings often set the tone for the whole day.

01:47.671 --> 01:50.872
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, Leo, what time do you wake up on weekdays?

01:51.624 --> 01:52.444
[SPEAKER_00]: Usually around 6.30.

01:53.585 --> 01:55.046
[SPEAKER_00]: Not too early, not too late.

01:55.706 --> 01:56.167
[SPEAKER_02]: Same here.

01:56.687 --> 02:00.089
[SPEAKER_02]: When I wake up, I try not to touch my phone right away.

02:00.709 --> 02:01.209
[SPEAKER_00]: Me too.

02:01.770 --> 02:05.652
[SPEAKER_00]: If I check messages immediately, my brain becomes noisy.

02:06.372 --> 02:07.032
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

02:07.052 --> 02:09.073
[SPEAKER_02]: I want a peaceful start.

02:09.534 --> 02:13.916
[SPEAKER_02]: So first, I sit up slowly and take three deep breaths.

02:14.636 --> 02:15.457
[SPEAKER_00]: That sounds nice.

02:16.017 --> 02:17.358
[SPEAKER_00]: It helps you focus, right?

02:18.050 --> 02:21.652
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, I just think, inhale, exhale.

02:22.232 --> 02:24.073
[SPEAKER_02]: It takes maybe 30 seconds.

02:24.774 --> 02:26.055
[SPEAKER_00]: My first step is simple.

02:26.555 --> 02:28.516
[SPEAKER_00]: I open the window for fresh air.

02:29.236 --> 02:30.177
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, I like that.

02:30.697 --> 02:33.119
[SPEAKER_02]: Fresh air can make you feel more awake.

02:33.779 --> 02:35.620
[SPEAKER_00]: Then I drink a glass of water.

02:36.180 --> 02:38.442
[SPEAKER_00]: After sleeping, my body feels dry.

02:39.142 --> 02:39.462
[SPEAKER_02]: Same.

02:40.003 --> 02:42.024
[SPEAKER_02]: Water is my first drink too.

02:42.524 --> 02:44.105
[SPEAKER_02]: It helps me feel refreshed.

02:44.774 --> 02:48.676
[SPEAKER_00]: Listeners, you can say, I drink a glass of water in the morning.

02:49.337 --> 02:50.057
[SPEAKER_02]: Great sentence.

02:50.738 --> 02:53.819
[SPEAKER_02]: After water, I go to the bathroom and wash my face.

02:54.500 --> 02:55.961
[SPEAKER_00]: Cold water or warm water?

02:56.781 --> 03:00.323
[SPEAKER_02]: Usually cool water, not ice cold, just cool.

03:00.864 --> 03:02.084
[SPEAKER_02]: It wakes me up gently.

03:02.785 --> 03:04.386
[SPEAKER_00]: I brush my teeth right away.

03:04.806 --> 03:05.687
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like a signal.

03:06.027 --> 03:07.748
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, the day starts now.

03:08.554 --> 03:08.794
[SPEAKER_02]: True.

03:09.414 --> 03:11.435
[SPEAKER_02]: After that, I do a short stretch.

03:11.936 --> 03:14.997
[SPEAKER_02]: Nothing hard, just my neck, shoulders, and back.

03:15.738 --> 03:17.078
[SPEAKER_00]: I do a little stretch, too.

03:17.599 --> 03:19.459
[SPEAKER_00]: If I don't, my body feels tight.

03:20.200 --> 03:22.941
[SPEAKER_02]: And stretching is also good English practice.

03:23.421 --> 03:26.363
[SPEAKER_02]: You can say, I stretch for five minutes.

03:27.163 --> 03:29.204
[SPEAKER_00]: Or I stretch my legs and my arms.

03:29.484 --> 03:30.085
[SPEAKER_00]: Very simple.

03:30.845 --> 03:32.666
[SPEAKER_02]: After stretching, I make the bed.

03:33.306 --> 03:33.586
[SPEAKER_00]: Really?

03:33.887 --> 03:34.267
[SPEAKER_00]: Every day?

03:34.983 --> 03:35.844
[SPEAKER_02]: Almost every day.

03:36.424 --> 03:40.706
[SPEAKER_02]: A made bed makes the room feel clean and my mind feels calm.

03:41.327 --> 03:42.247
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a nice idea.

03:42.888 --> 03:44.669
[SPEAKER_00]: My next step is a quick shower.

03:45.369 --> 03:46.930
[SPEAKER_02]: Morning shower is great.

03:47.290 --> 03:48.551
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you take a long shower?

03:49.251 --> 03:50.032
[SPEAKER_00]: No, short.

03:50.232 --> 03:51.853
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe five to seven minutes.

03:52.053 --> 03:52.953
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't want to waste time.

03:53.654 --> 03:56.155
[SPEAKER_02]: After I wash up, I think about breakfast.

03:56.616 --> 03:59.137
[SPEAKER_02]: For me, breakfast is small but steady.

03:59.697 --> 04:00.618
[SPEAKER_00]: What do you usually eat?

04:01.359 --> 04:05.162
[SPEAKER_02]: Often oatmeal, yogurt, fruit, or toast with eggs.

04:05.703 --> 04:06.464
[SPEAKER_02]: Nothing fancy.

04:07.124 --> 04:08.105
[SPEAKER_00]: Same style for me.

04:08.525 --> 04:11.108
[SPEAKER_00]: I like eggs, toast, and maybe a banana.

04:11.788 --> 04:14.050
[SPEAKER_02]: While I eat, I try to be present.

04:14.511 --> 04:15.772
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't scroll a lot.

04:16.352 --> 04:17.874
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a mindful breakfast.

04:18.554 --> 04:18.835
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes.

04:19.455 --> 04:22.578
[SPEAKER_02]: Mindful means you pay attention to the moment.

04:23.018 --> 04:24.139
[SPEAKER_02]: You taste the food.

04:24.540 --> 04:25.561
[SPEAKER_02]: You feel your body.

04:26.193 --> 04:26.713
[SPEAKER_00]: I like that.

04:27.274 --> 04:29.515
[SPEAKER_00]: I also play quiet music sometimes.

04:29.755 --> 04:31.996
[SPEAKER_00]: It can influence my mood in a good way.

04:32.696 --> 04:33.177
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

04:33.697 --> 04:36.078
[SPEAKER_02]: Music can change the feeling in the room.

04:36.799 --> 04:40.561
[SPEAKER_00]: After breakfast, I look at my day and choose my top tasks.

04:41.381 --> 04:41.701
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes.

04:42.142 --> 04:44.023
[SPEAKER_02]: This is when I prioritize.

04:44.683 --> 04:48.105
[SPEAKER_00]: Prioritize means choose what is most important first.

04:48.905 --> 04:49.125
[SPEAKER_02]: Right.

04:49.625 --> 04:54.008
[SPEAKER_02]: I ask myself, what are the three most important tasks today?

04:54.672 --> 04:56.393
[SPEAKER_00]: And you don't need ten tasks.

04:56.733 --> 04:58.054
[SPEAKER_00]: Three is enough to start.

04:58.794 --> 04:59.354
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

04:59.934 --> 05:01.095
[SPEAKER_02]: Then I write them down.

05:01.615 --> 05:03.636
[SPEAKER_02]: Writing helps my brain relax.

05:04.376 --> 05:05.417
[SPEAKER_00]: I do something similar.

05:05.777 --> 05:08.158
[SPEAKER_00]: I make a short plan, not a long plan.

05:08.838 --> 05:12.240
[SPEAKER_02]: Also, listeners, here are two useful sentences.

05:12.880 --> 05:15.081
[SPEAKER_02]: Today, I want to finish my work.

05:15.801 --> 05:18.823
[SPEAKER_00]: Today, I want to practice English for ten minutes.

05:19.583 --> 05:19.883
[SPEAKER_02]: Nice!

05:20.503 --> 05:23.565
[SPEAKER_02]: And you can also say, my first task is...

05:24.287 --> 05:29.731
[SPEAKER_00]: Good, now one more morning habit I love, a short walk, even five minutes.

05:30.471 --> 05:33.993
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, a small walk gives you light and fresh air.

05:34.614 --> 05:36.975
[SPEAKER_00]: And it helps you feel ready to work or study.

05:37.636 --> 05:39.737
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, so that's our morning routine.

05:40.197 --> 05:45.761
[SPEAKER_00]: Breathe, water, wash, stretch, breakfast, and a small plan.

05:46.521 --> 05:50.444
[SPEAKER_02]: Simple, small steps, but they can shape the whole day.

05:51.198 --> 05:55.360
[SPEAKER_00]: Now let's move on to the afternoon, because afternoons can be tricky.

05:55.981 --> 05:58.802
[SPEAKER_02]: Leo, do you ever feel tired in the afternoon?

05:59.463 --> 06:00.103
[SPEAKER_00]: All the time.

06:00.523 --> 06:02.644
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the famous afternoon slump.

06:03.425 --> 06:03.765
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes.

06:04.365 --> 06:08.407
[SPEAKER_02]: Slump means you suddenly feel low energy and slow.

06:09.068 --> 06:13.170
[SPEAKER_00]: For me, the slump often comes around 2 p.m. or 3 p.m.

06:13.870 --> 06:14.151
[SPEAKER_02]: Same.

06:14.651 --> 06:16.432
[SPEAKER_02]: So what do you do to handle it?

06:17.166 --> 06:19.467
[SPEAKER_00]: First, I try to eat a balanced lunch.

06:19.927 --> 06:22.569
[SPEAKER_00]: If I eat too much fried food, I feel sleepy.

06:23.309 --> 06:23.649
[SPEAKER_02]: Same.

06:24.190 --> 06:28.592
[SPEAKER_02]: I try to have rice or bread plus protein plus vegetables.

06:29.292 --> 06:32.794
[SPEAKER_02]: Listeners, you can say, I eat a balanced lunch.

06:33.515 --> 06:33.815
[SPEAKER_00]: Great.

06:34.475 --> 06:37.977
[SPEAKER_00]: After lunch, I do one short thing before I sit down again.

06:38.697 --> 06:39.138
[SPEAKER_02]: Like what?

06:39.698 --> 06:43.940
[SPEAKER_00]: A short walk, or I clean the table, or I wash a few dishes.

06:44.360 --> 06:45.601
[SPEAKER_00]: It helps my body move.

06:46.453 --> 06:48.334
[SPEAKER_02]: Movement helps you wake up again.

06:48.974 --> 06:50.934
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, and it also helps digestion.

06:51.474 --> 06:53.115
[SPEAKER_00]: Then I sit down and work.

06:53.895 --> 06:58.677
[SPEAKER_02]: In the afternoon, I use a simple method, work in short blocks.

06:59.397 --> 07:01.617
[SPEAKER_00]: Like 25 minutes and then a short break?

07:02.338 --> 07:02.818
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

07:03.418 --> 07:06.659
[SPEAKER_02]: I work 25 minutes, then break 5 minutes.

07:07.319 --> 07:10.380
[SPEAKER_00]: During the break, I stand up and stretch my shoulders.

07:11.300 --> 07:13.601
[SPEAKER_02]: I also drink water, or I make tea.

07:14.369 --> 07:18.052
[SPEAKER_00]: A warm drink can feel calming and it can help you recharge.

07:18.812 --> 07:21.475
[SPEAKER_02]: Recharge means get your energy back.

07:22.175 --> 07:22.415
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

07:22.956 --> 07:25.257
[SPEAKER_00]: And during breaks, I try to be mindful.

07:25.958 --> 07:27.920
[SPEAKER_02]: So you don't scroll mindlessly?

07:28.600 --> 07:29.301
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

07:29.701 --> 07:32.283
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't want my break to become a stress break.

07:33.044 --> 07:33.544
[SPEAKER_02]: Good point.

07:33.984 --> 07:37.047
[SPEAKER_02]: Sometimes social media makes the slump worse.

07:37.767 --> 07:38.188
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

07:38.688 --> 07:40.549
[SPEAKER_00]: So I choose a clean break.

07:41.050 --> 07:43.772
[SPEAKER_00]: I look out the window or do slow breathing.

07:44.514 --> 07:46.656
[SPEAKER_02]: Let's give listeners a simple phrase.

07:47.317 --> 07:48.818
[SPEAKER_02]: I take a short break.

07:49.519 --> 07:51.781
[SPEAKER_00]: And another, I need to recharge.

07:52.541 --> 07:52.842
[SPEAKER_02]: Nice.

07:53.442 --> 07:56.565
[SPEAKER_02]: Also, I prioritize again in the afternoon.

07:57.225 --> 07:58.186
[SPEAKER_00]: You do it twice?

07:58.927 --> 08:02.310
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, because mornings and afternoons can be different.

08:02.991 --> 08:05.373
[SPEAKER_00]: True, sometimes new tasks appear.

08:06.133 --> 08:10.838
[SPEAKER_02]: So I ask, what is the most important thing to finish before dinner?

08:11.465 --> 08:12.045
[SPEAKER_00]: That's smart.

08:12.626 --> 08:14.587
[SPEAKER_00]: One clear goal helps you focus.

08:15.347 --> 08:17.889
[SPEAKER_02]: And focus is hard when you feel tired.

08:18.570 --> 08:18.930
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

08:19.370 --> 08:20.771
[SPEAKER_00]: So I also try this.

08:21.231 --> 08:24.413
[SPEAKER_00]: I do the easiest task first, just to start moving.

08:25.094 --> 08:25.974
[SPEAKER_02]: That's a nice trick.

08:26.435 --> 08:28.576
[SPEAKER_02]: Starting is often the hardest part.

08:29.276 --> 08:29.877
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

08:30.297 --> 08:31.718
[SPEAKER_00]: Once you start, you feel better.

08:32.418 --> 08:34.480
[SPEAKER_02]: Another thing that helps me is sunlight.

08:34.960 --> 08:37.722
[SPEAKER_02]: If possible, I step outside for two minutes.

08:38.440 --> 08:40.301
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, even two minutes can help.

08:40.842 --> 08:43.184
[SPEAKER_00]: When I come back, I feel a little more awake.

08:43.924 --> 08:47.247
[SPEAKER_02]: Now let's talk about language practice in the afternoon.

08:47.907 --> 08:48.468
[SPEAKER_00]: Good idea.

08:48.948 --> 08:51.350
[SPEAKER_00]: Many people want to practice, but they forget.

08:52.070 --> 08:53.952
[SPEAKER_02]: So here's a small routine.

08:54.492 --> 08:57.715
[SPEAKER_02]: Choose one sentence and repeat it five times.

08:58.455 --> 09:02.458
[SPEAKER_00]: For example, I'm feeling a little tired, so I will take a break.

09:03.199 --> 09:03.920
[SPEAKER_02]: Great sentence.

09:04.720 --> 09:06.462
[SPEAKER_00]: Or I will focus for 20 minutes.

09:07.502 --> 09:07.802
[SPEAKER_02]: Nice!

09:08.422 --> 09:10.763
[SPEAKER_02]: Small practice, but daily practice.

09:11.503 --> 09:14.804
[SPEAKER_00]: And daily practice influences your progress a lot.

09:15.564 --> 09:16.244
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

09:16.725 --> 09:19.025
[SPEAKER_02]: Your English grows little by little.

09:19.805 --> 09:28.988
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, so afternoon tips are balanced lunch, short movement, clear priorities, mindful breaks, and recharge time.

09:29.734 --> 09:30.234
[SPEAKER_02]: Perfect.

09:30.815 --> 09:37.341
[SPEAKER_02]: Now let's go to the evening routine because evenings help us change from work mode to rest mode.

09:38.021 --> 09:40.063
[SPEAKER_02]: Leo, what do you do after work?

09:40.724 --> 09:42.785
[SPEAKER_00]: The first thing I do is a quick review.

09:43.546 --> 09:45.067
[SPEAKER_02]: Like checking what you finished?

09:45.708 --> 09:46.008
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

09:46.429 --> 09:49.872
[SPEAKER_00]: I look at my list and I say, okay, I did these things.

09:50.612 --> 09:51.373
[SPEAKER_02]: That's important.

09:51.693 --> 09:53.395
[SPEAKER_02]: It gives you a small win.

09:54.115 --> 09:56.818
[SPEAKER_00]: And if something is not finished, I move it to tomorrow.

09:57.533 --> 09:57.813
[SPEAKER_02]: Great!

09:58.334 --> 10:00.396
[SPEAKER_02]: That helps your brain stop worrying.

10:01.036 --> 10:01.417
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

10:01.937 --> 10:03.499
[SPEAKER_00]: Then I need a transition.

10:04.239 --> 10:08.103
[SPEAKER_02]: Transition means a change from one activity to another.

10:08.783 --> 10:09.284
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

10:09.824 --> 10:15.269
[SPEAKER_00]: If I finish work and jump straight into dinner with no break, I still feel like I'm working.

10:15.990 --> 10:18.012
[SPEAKER_02]: So what transition do you like?

10:18.733 --> 10:23.137
[SPEAKER_00]: A short walk, or light exercise, or even changing clothes.

10:23.898 --> 10:25.438
[SPEAKER_02]: I love changing clothes too.

10:25.979 --> 10:28.279
[SPEAKER_02]: It tells my brain work is done.

10:28.299 --> 10:30.380
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a good transition habit.

10:31.080 --> 10:33.741
[SPEAKER_02]: I also do a little tidy up in the room.

10:34.261 --> 10:36.342
[SPEAKER_02]: Not perfect cleaning, just simple.

10:37.002 --> 10:40.023
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, a clean space can influence your mood.

10:40.723 --> 10:41.223
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

10:41.763 --> 10:42.944
[SPEAKER_02]: Then dinner time.

10:43.404 --> 10:43.944
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you cook?

10:44.664 --> 10:45.525
[SPEAKER_00]: Sometimes I cook.

10:45.745 --> 10:47.025
[SPEAKER_00]: Sometimes I keep it simple.

10:47.505 --> 10:50.246
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't want dinner to become a big stress project.

10:50.826 --> 10:51.226
[SPEAKER_01]: Same.

10:51.526 --> 10:53.467
[SPEAKER_01]: Dinner can be simple and still healthy.

10:53.845 --> 10:56.267
[SPEAKER_00]: I try to eat without my phone in my hand.

10:56.687 --> 10:57.147
[SPEAKER_01]: Me too.

10:57.448 --> 10:58.769
[SPEAKER_01]: I want a mindful meal.

10:59.269 --> 11:02.611
[SPEAKER_00]: And after dinner, I take five minutes to clean the kitchen area.

11:03.012 --> 11:03.972
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a great habit.

11:04.373 --> 11:06.814
[SPEAKER_01]: When you wake up, you feel better if the kitchen is clean.

11:07.255 --> 11:09.756
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, it saves time in the morning routine.

11:10.217 --> 11:13.299
[SPEAKER_01]: After dinner, I like to do something relaxing, but not too heavy.

11:13.659 --> 11:14.099
[SPEAKER_01]: Like what?

11:14.520 --> 11:16.641
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes I read a short book chapter.

11:17.122 --> 11:18.683
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes I watch a light show.

11:19.063 --> 11:21.745
[SPEAKER_00]: I like listening to a podcast or calling a friend.

11:22.321 --> 11:22.821
[SPEAKER_01]: That's nice.

11:23.061 --> 11:25.862
[SPEAKER_01]: Talking to a friend can help you recharge emotionally.

11:26.262 --> 11:28.563
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, and it can make you feel connected.

11:29.003 --> 11:31.344
[SPEAKER_01]: Also, evenings are good for gentle learning.

11:31.724 --> 11:32.965
[SPEAKER_00]: Like English listening?

11:33.405 --> 11:33.925
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

11:34.265 --> 11:35.285
[SPEAKER_01]: Ten minutes is enough.

11:35.726 --> 11:37.686
[SPEAKER_00]: Listeners, here's an easy sentence.

11:38.166 --> 11:40.767
[SPEAKER_00]: In the evening, I relax and recharge.

11:41.248 --> 11:41.748
[SPEAKER_01]: Great.

11:41.968 --> 11:42.408
[SPEAKER_01]: And another.

11:42.988 --> 11:45.069
[SPEAKER_01]: After work, I need a transition.

11:45.489 --> 11:45.869
[SPEAKER_00]: Nice.

11:46.149 --> 11:48.110
[SPEAKER_00]: You're using vocabulary in real life.

11:48.608 --> 11:50.009
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, that's the goal.

11:50.389 --> 11:51.950
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you exercise in the evening?

11:52.410 --> 11:54.211
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes, light exercise.

11:54.611 --> 11:56.993
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe a walk, yoga, or stretching.

11:57.453 --> 11:59.754
[SPEAKER_00]: I try to move a little, not too much.

12:00.175 --> 12:02.656
[SPEAKER_00]: If I exercise too late, I can't sleep well.

12:03.076 --> 12:03.376
[SPEAKER_01]: True.

12:03.757 --> 12:04.617
[SPEAKER_01]: Everyone is different.

12:05.017 --> 12:07.799
[SPEAKER_01]: The key is to notice what influences your sleep.

12:08.199 --> 12:08.779
[SPEAKER_00]: Great point.

12:09.180 --> 12:11.581
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, now let's move to the night routine.

12:12.121 --> 12:14.903
[SPEAKER_01]: Night routines help us slow down and sleep better.

12:15.468 --> 12:18.510
[SPEAKER_00]: Night routine is my favorite part because it's quiet.

12:19.011 --> 12:19.371
[SPEAKER_01]: Same.

12:19.791 --> 12:23.634
[SPEAKER_01]: For me, the night routine starts around 9 p.m. What do you do first?

12:24.195 --> 12:25.176
[SPEAKER_01]: I dim the lights.

12:25.436 --> 12:27.597
[SPEAKER_01]: I want softer light, not bright light.

12:27.978 --> 12:28.298
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

12:28.598 --> 12:31.000
[SPEAKER_00]: Bright light tells your brain to stay awake.

12:31.520 --> 12:32.882
[SPEAKER_01]: Then I put my phone away.

12:33.282 --> 12:35.023
[SPEAKER_01]: Not always perfect, but I try.

12:35.504 --> 12:38.065
[SPEAKER_00]: I also keep my phone a little far from my bed.

12:38.466 --> 12:38.826
[SPEAKER_01]: Great.

12:39.286 --> 12:42.509
[SPEAKER_01]: Next, I wash my face and change into comfortable clothes.

12:42.905 --> 12:45.747
[SPEAKER_00]: That step makes me feel clean and ready for rest.

12:46.247 --> 12:46.547
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

12:47.127 --> 12:51.170
[SPEAKER_01]: Then I do something calm, reading or writing a few lines.

12:51.610 --> 12:52.751
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you write about your day?

12:53.191 --> 12:53.711
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes.

12:54.232 --> 12:56.253
[SPEAKER_01]: I write one good thing that happened.

12:56.613 --> 12:58.054
[SPEAKER_00]: That sounds like gratitude.

12:58.514 --> 12:59.254
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

12:59.555 --> 13:01.756
[SPEAKER_01]: Gratitude means feeling thankful.

13:02.216 --> 13:02.937
[SPEAKER_00]: I love that.

13:02.957 --> 13:05.338
[SPEAKER_00]: I do a small gratitude moment too.

13:05.778 --> 13:06.479
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you write?

13:06.919 --> 13:08.120
[SPEAKER_00]: I write one sentence.

13:08.560 --> 13:12.062
[SPEAKER_00]: Today I am thankful for... Beautiful and simple.

13:12.470 --> 13:15.553
[SPEAKER_00]: For example, today I am thankful for a good lunch.

13:16.374 --> 13:18.896
[SPEAKER_01]: Or I am thankful for a calm walk.

13:19.436 --> 13:21.879
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, it helps my mind feel lighter.

13:22.379 --> 13:24.701
[SPEAKER_01]: After that, I prepare for tomorrow a little.

13:25.122 --> 13:26.663
[SPEAKER_00]: Like clothes or a bag.

13:27.023 --> 13:30.426
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, I put out my clothes or I pack my bag.

13:30.827 --> 13:34.490
[SPEAKER_00]: That reduces morning stress and it protects your morning routine.

13:34.951 --> 13:35.751
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

13:36.252 --> 13:38.454
[SPEAKER_01]: Then I set my alarm and get into bed.

13:38.843 --> 13:40.183
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you do anything in bed?

13:40.603 --> 13:42.024
[SPEAKER_01]: I try not to use my phone.

13:42.304 --> 13:44.484
[SPEAKER_01]: I do two minutes of slow breathing.

13:44.864 --> 13:47.985
[SPEAKER_00]: That helps you focus on your body and calm your thoughts.

13:48.505 --> 13:53.687
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, and if my mind is busy, I tell myself, I can think about this tomorrow.

13:54.047 --> 13:54.867
[SPEAKER_00]: Good self-talk.

13:55.367 --> 13:56.307
[SPEAKER_01]: Then sleep.

13:56.887 --> 13:59.628
[SPEAKER_01]: And I try to sleep at a similar time each night.

14:00.028 --> 14:02.929
[SPEAKER_00]: Because consistency influences sleep quality.

14:03.361 --> 14:04.182
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

14:04.602 --> 14:07.243
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, Leo, let's do a short recap for listeners.

14:07.643 --> 14:07.884
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

14:08.524 --> 14:13.567
[SPEAKER_00]: Morning, water, wash, stretch, breakfast, and a small plan.

14:14.027 --> 14:21.111
[SPEAKER_01]: Afternoon, balanced lunch, short movement, clear priorities, mindful breaks, and recharge time.

14:21.592 --> 14:28.233
[SPEAKER_00]: Evening, review your day, transition out of work, eat calmly, and do something relaxing.

14:28.753 --> 14:34.815
[SPEAKER_01]: Night, dim lights, put the phone away, gratitude, prepare for tomorrow, and sleep.

14:35.335 --> 14:38.435
[SPEAKER_00]: Simple, not perfect, but simple and repeatable.

14:38.956 --> 14:41.956
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, let's do a short speaking practice with our listeners.

14:42.636 --> 14:43.957
[SPEAKER_01]: Listeners, repeat after me.

14:44.477 --> 14:47.077
[SPEAKER_01]: My daily routine helps me feel calm.

14:47.537 --> 14:47.917
[SPEAKER_00]: Repeat.

14:48.357 --> 14:51.058
[SPEAKER_00]: My daily routine helps me feel calm.

14:51.560 --> 14:51.840
[SPEAKER_01]: Good.

14:52.320 --> 14:55.622
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, I prioritize my top three tasks.

14:56.143 --> 14:56.543
[SPEAKER_00]: Repeat.

14:56.983 --> 14:59.585
[SPEAKER_00]: I prioritize my top three tasks.

15:00.085 --> 15:00.545
[SPEAKER_01]: Great.

15:01.166 --> 15:04.468
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, I take a mindful break to recharge.

15:04.928 --> 15:05.328
[SPEAKER_00]: Repeat.

15:05.789 --> 15:08.310
[SPEAKER_00]: I take a mindful break to recharge.

15:08.910 --> 15:09.411
[SPEAKER_01]: Excellent.

15:09.971 --> 15:11.912
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, answer this question out loud.

15:12.393 --> 15:13.433
[SPEAKER_01]: What time do you wake up?

15:13.914 --> 15:14.914
[SPEAKER_00]: Use this pattern.

15:15.374 --> 15:18.256
[SPEAKER_01]: I wake up at... And one more question.

15:18.816 --> 15:21.038
[SPEAKER_01]: What helps you avoid the afternoon slump?

15:21.640 --> 15:22.580
[SPEAKER_00]: Use this pattern.

15:23.160 --> 15:24.561
[SPEAKER_00]: A mm helps me.

15:25.001 --> 15:27.521
[SPEAKER_00]: For example, a short walk helps me.

15:27.961 --> 15:28.341
[SPEAKER_01]: Great.

15:28.801 --> 15:29.582
[SPEAKER_01]: Last question.

15:30.022 --> 15:31.582
[SPEAKER_01]: What is your night routine like?

15:32.142 --> 15:33.202
[SPEAKER_00]: Use this pattern.

15:33.642 --> 15:38.203
[SPEAKER_00]: At night, I... For example, at night, I read for 10 minutes.

15:38.763 --> 15:39.363
[SPEAKER_01]: Perfect.

15:39.804 --> 15:42.604
[SPEAKER_01]: Practice is more important than perfect grammar.

15:43.004 --> 15:43.324
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

15:43.724 --> 15:46.965
[SPEAKER_00]: Speak slowly, speak clearly, keep going.

15:47.483 --> 15:52.250
[SPEAKER_01]: And don't forget, our free PDF has extra questions and exercises for this episode.

15:52.631 --> 15:52.911
[SPEAKER_00]: Great.

15:53.512 --> 15:55.775
[SPEAKER_00]: Now it's time for the vocabulary review.

15:56.316 --> 15:58.039
[SPEAKER_01]: Vocabulary review section.

15:58.459 --> 16:00.643
[SPEAKER_01]: Here are about 10 words we use today.

16:01.043 --> 16:03.467
[SPEAKER_01]: Each word has a meaning and an example sentence.

16:04.050 --> 16:08.574
[SPEAKER_00]: One, routine, meaning the regular things you do each day.

16:09.174 --> 16:13.057
[SPEAKER_00]: Example, my morning routine starts with water and stretching.

16:13.397 --> 16:17.720
[SPEAKER_00]: Two, influence, meaning to affect something or change it.

16:18.221 --> 16:22.104
[SPEAKER_00]: Example, quiet music can influence my mood in the morning.

16:22.524 --> 16:27.408
[SPEAKER_00]: Three, refreshed, meaning feeling clean and full of energy again.

16:27.908 --> 16:31.370
[SPEAKER_00]: Example, after I wash my face, I feel refreshed.

16:31.931 --> 16:33.092
[SPEAKER_00]: Four, focus.

16:33.532 --> 16:36.073
[SPEAKER_00]: Meaning, to pay attention to one thing.

16:36.513 --> 16:40.015
[SPEAKER_00]: Example, I focus on my work for 25 minutes.

16:40.475 --> 16:42.056
[SPEAKER_00]: Five, prioritize.

16:42.416 --> 16:45.418
[SPEAKER_00]: Meaning, to choose what is most important first.

16:45.898 --> 16:49.940
[SPEAKER_00]: Example, I prioritize my top three tasks every day.

16:50.860 --> 16:54.963
[SPEAKER_00]: 6.

16:51.281 --> 16:54.963
[SPEAKER_00]: Slump Meaning a time when you feel tired and slow.

16:55.424 --> 17:00.047
[SPEAKER_00]: Example, I sometimes have an afternoon slump after lunch.

17:00.067 --> 17:00.047
7.

17:00.507 --> 17:04.550
[SPEAKER_00]: Mindful Meaning paying attention to the present moment.

17:05.011 --> 17:08.873
[SPEAKER_00]: Example, I take a mindful break and drink tea slowly.

17:08.893 --> 17:08.873
8.

17:09.934 --> 17:13.036
[SPEAKER_00]: Recharge Meaning to get energy again.

17:13.537 --> 17:15.178
[SPEAKER_00]: Example, a short walk

17:15.538 --> 17:19.561
[SPEAKER_00]: helps me recharge in the afternoon.

17:20.001 --> 17:23.003
[SPEAKER_00]: Meaning, a change from one activity to another.

17:23.503 --> 17:28.987
[SPEAKER_00]: Example, changing clothes helps my transition from work to rest.

17:30.488 --> 17:32.889
[SPEAKER_00]: Meaning, a feeling of being thankful.

17:33.350 --> 17:37.192
[SPEAKER_00]: Example, I write one line of gratitude before I sleep.

17:37.592 --> 17:38.293
[SPEAKER_01]: Great job!

17:38.693 --> 17:41.815
[SPEAKER_01]: Try to use two of these words in your own sentences today.

17:42.227 --> 17:46.613
[SPEAKER_00]: And remember, small routines create big change over time.

17:47.073 --> 17:49.957
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you for listening to English Podcast 1314.

17:51.098 --> 17:54.122
[SPEAKER_00]: Keep learning, keep practicing, and take care.

17:54.623 --> 17:55.304
[SPEAKER_01]: See you next time.

